WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.552
Small text differences can create fake categories.

00:00:03.552 --> 00:00:09.767
In this example, the channel column contains values such as Web with spaces, EMAIL in uppercase,

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:13.257
web in lowercase, and Store with spaces.

00:00:13.257 --> 00:00:15.262
The status column has the same problem.

00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:21.282
We use the string accessor, str, to apply text methods to the entire column.

00:00:21.282 --> 00:00:24.332
Strip removes leading and trailing spaces.

00:00:24.332 --> 00:00:27.587
Lower converts the text to lowercase.

00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:33.492
Click Run and then look at value_counts for channel.

00:00:33.492 --> 00:00:38.187
Before cleaning, values that mean the same thing could be counted separately.

00:00:38.187 --> 00:00:45.232
After cleaning, Web, web, and values with extra spaces become one consistent category.

00:00:45.232 --> 00:00:48.627
This matters for groupby, pivot tables, charts, and machine-learning features.

00:00:48.627 --> 00:00:52.237
Be careful, however, not to standardize blindly.

00:00:52.237 --> 00:00:56.582
Sometimes capitalization or spacing is meaningful.

00:00:56.582 --> 00:01:02.697
The goal is to create consistent categories based on the meaning of the data,

00:01:02.697 --> 00:01:06.319
not simply to modify text because a method is available.
